# 17. DEPLOYMENT AND AUTOMATIC UPDATES

The project must have a setup.sh script.

It must support automatic deployment when a new authorized GitHub push happens.

Required behavior:

1. Receive a GitHub push webhook.
2. Verify that the webhook is legitimate.
3. Pull the latest repository changes.
4. Check whether setup.sh has changed.
5. If setup.sh has changed, update it safely and execute the new version before continuing.
6. Check whether required software is installed.
7. Check the required configurations.
8. Check cron jobs and webhook configuration.
9. Check Caddy configuration if Caddy is used.
10. Load configuration values from a JSON configuration file where appropriate.
11. Update the cron script if the repository version has changed.
12. Build the application.
13. Start or restart the application using PM2.

The setup process must be safe to execute more than once.

Do not use an unsafe self-deleting script update method.

Use an atomic update and process replacement/re-execution strategy.

Do not allow an unauthorized GitHub webhook to deploy arbitrary code.

The setup process must log failures and stop if a critical operation fails.

Never silently start the old application and claim that the new version was deployed successfully.

